+(resuming-a-shuffled-epoch)=
+### Resuming a Shuffled Epoch
+
+When a job is interrupted mid-epoch, a new {class}`~torch.utils.data.RandomSampler`
+would shuffle afresh, so the restarted job would see some samples twice and miss
+others. To avoid that, save the sampler's
+{meth}`~torch.utils.data.RandomSampler.state_dict` with the rest of the checkpoint
+and pass it to {meth}`~torch.utils.data.RandomSampler.load_state_dict` of the
+sampler in the restarted job. Its next epoch then yields exactly the indices the
+interrupted epoch had not yet yielded, in the same order, and later epochs are
+shuffled as usual:
+
+```python
+loader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=32, shuffle=True)
+start_epoch = 0
+if checkpoint is not None:  # restarting: resume the interrupted epoch
+    loader.sampler.load_state_dict(checkpoint["sampler"])
+    start_epoch = checkpoint["epoch"]
+for epoch in range(start_epoch, num_epochs):
+    for batch in loader:
+        train_step(batch)
+        if should_checkpoint():
+            torch.save(
+                {"sampler": loader.sampler.state_dict(), "epoch": epoch},
+                "checkpoint.pt",
+            )
+```
+
+The sampler must be over data of the same length, and with the same
+`replacement` and `num_samples`, as the one that was checkpointed. If it has a
+`generator`, the resumed epoch leaves the generator in the state the
+uninterrupted run would have left it in.
+
+:::{warning}
+The state records the indices the sampler has *yielded*. With `num_workers > 0`,
+a {class}`~torch.utils.data.DataLoader` draws indices for up to
+`prefetch_factor * num_workers` batches ahead of the batches it has returned, so
+checkpointing the sampler mid-epoch then skips the samples of those prefetched
+batches on resume. The state is exact when `num_workers=0`.
+:::
+
